    {
      "slug": "coltsville",
      "title": "Coltsville",
      "description": "",
      "featured": true,
      "cover": null,
      "order": 8,
      "type": "curated",
      "place": "Hartford",
      "collage": true,
      "caption": "In 1848, Samuel Colt, inventor of the automatic revolver, built one of America's first company towns around the Colt Manufacturing Company's main factory near downtown Hartford. Enshrined in 2014 as the Coltsville National Historic District, the complex consists of the old factory (the “Armory”), currently being restored and transitioned to lofts; Colt’s sprawling Italian villa, Armsmear (literally “meadow of arms”); Colt Park, a city park on land donated to the City of Hartford by Colt’s widow, Elizabeth; a variety of tenement housing built to house the largely European immigrant workforce that made Colt’s ingenious pistols and rifles; and a row of ornate, if somewhat desolate, Victorian-era mansions originally owned by Colt and his affiliates.\n\nThe Armory itself has a long history of burning and redevelopment. Much of the original manufacturing complex is gone, but good samaritans have seen to it that its astonishing onion-shaped, deep blue dome – personally designed by Samuel Colt – has remained intact. Today, the dome and the “rampant colt” placed at its apex are a ubiquitous symbol of Hartford, though they are most visible from I-91, the six-lane highway that cuts through Downtown Hartford and permanently severs the city’s connection to its raison d’etre, the Connecticut River.\n\nColt’s story is just one of many integral to the Connecticut capital, but it is exemplary of the historical forces that shaped the city and the nation. Hartford’s early ascendance was underwritten by the profits from Colt’s pistols, and arms manufacturing itself is a deeply Yankee trade: having arrived in the New World to find not a paradise but fickle, even hostile New England soil, Europe’s enterprising refugee Anglos turned to the rivers for power and the Natives for blood.\n\nColt’s big break came in 1847 when the US federal government enlisted his help in manufacturing 1,000 revolvers for the Mexican-American War, one of the new nation’s most nakedly imperialist undertakings. After securing the title to the entirety of the American West, the government set about depopulating the land, eradicating Native Americans and replacing them with distinct European populations that would eventually come to be known simply as Americans (e.g., Czechs and Germans in Texas, Norwegians and Swedes in the Upper Midwest, and even Basque ranchers in Idaho).\n\nIt was Colt’s Single Action Army revolver, aka the Peacemaker, that meted out justice in the lawless West. Its cousin, the Winchester Lever-action Rifle, was being manufactured just down the Long Island Sound in New Haven, and together, they became known as the “gun(s) that won the West.” For 20 long, violent years, until 1892, the Peacemaker was the revolver of choice for the US Army.\n\nColt would not live to see those days. His last memories were likely of Colt’s meteoric ascension during the Civil War, when he sold his namesake arms to both Union and Confederate troops. But it was not only Colt’s amorality that allowed him to amass a fortune worthy of a Venetian villa. He displayed throughout his life an uncanny ability to marshal the burgeoning forces of industrial production for the furthering of private enterprise. Most notably, he adopted principles from the Federal Armory in Springfield, 30 miles up the Connecticut River, to hasten and standardize production. The development of interchangeable parts in Springfield arguably heralded the zenith of the American Industrial Revolution, and Colt harvested its bounty to design some of the most advanced industrial processes of his age, presaging Henry Ford’s Detroit assembly lines 50 years later.",
