    {
      "slug": "the-stag",
      "title": "The Stag",
      "description": "",
      "featured": false,
      "cover": null,
      "order": 0,
      "type": "curated",
      "place": "Hartford",
      "place_order": 5,
      "caption": "Hartford is the great Yankee city of yore. Like all other industrial New England cities, its history and culture has been heavily shaped by domestic and international migration – most notably, today, Puerto Rican migration. But Hartford had the unique fortune of being home both to defense manufacturing institutions and globe-bestriding financial institutions. It became by the mid-twentieth century a global nexus for the manufacture of violent objects as well as financial instruments insuring capital against loss. This led to the creation of a singularly powerful WASP establishment, known locally as “The Bishops,” who wielded power over the city’s division of labor as well as the nation’s emergent financial networks. Other New England cities simply don’t fit the bill. Boston is and was a port city – outward facing, global, and likely too Irish (though Hartford has plenty of Irish history as well). Worcester is firmly a blue collar city – in much the same way as Hartford – but it never developed a parallel white collar ecosystem as robust as Hartford’s.\n\nOne can imagine Hartford as it was at its inception: a verdant, bowl-shaped depression tucked among the rolling hills and ancient forests of New England. Rich with the archetypal flora and fauna of America – evergreens, maples, deer and bears – the natural world provided the perfect fodder for WASP self-mythologizing. As a result, Hartford’s built environment is littered with the remains of, for lack of a better word, the effluent of the local illuminati. Stags front the bizarre clubhouses that housed the city’s elite social organizations. Stags are featured prominently on the extraordinary Elizabethan-style public schools of inner Hartford, once some of the highest-achieving schools in the nation. The Hartford, one of Hartford’s preeminent insurance companies, features a Stag on its logo.\n\nCuriously, however, the Stag is not an organic symbol of Hartford. The first Hartford Stag was the one on the city’s official seal, guaranteeing ungulate ubiquity for the city’s foreseeable future. But that Stag came from the Stag that is the symbol of Hertford, England. So it was not, initially, the Wapiti that gave Hartford its logo but the great Yankee tradition of aping English culture.\n\nThe Stag entity was subsequently transposed, modified, and extended, perhaps most famously onto the logo of The Hartford. But The Hartford’s logo itself came from The Monarch of the Glen, a glorious 1851 painting made by the English artist Edwin Landseer (more points for sophisticated English names) depicting a resolute Stag in front of Scottish highland scenery. Hilariously, the Monarch of the Glen Wikipedia page contains an entire subsection dedicated to “Modern derivatives and corporate logos.”\n\nThis is Yankeedom at its finest – assumed inheritance of traditions of Olde England, applied to the deeply individualistic, economical genius of the first Americans. "
